Subject: gl: serve the anchor's limits to WebGL on every backend

`scripts/checks/gl-caps-check.mjs` passes for every anchor on the shipped
linux-x64 artifact. On the macOS one it passes only for the anchor measured
on Apple silicon, where the claim and the host are the same numbers, and
fails for every cross-backend anchor. On an Apple M4 Max under
`--fingerprint-anchor=windows-d3d11-nvidia` the renderer string is the
anchor's and the capability table is Apple's:

  ALIASED_POINT_SIZE_RANGE           served [1, 511]      anchor [1, 1024]
  MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS                  served 16384         anchor 32767
  MAX_VERTEX_UNIFORM_VECTORS         served 1024          anchor 4095
  MAX_VERTEX_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS      served 4096          anchor 16380
  MAX_SAMPLES                        served 4             anchor 8
  UNIFORM_BUFFER_OFFSET_ALIGNMENT    served 16            anchor 256

A claimed GeForce beside `UNIFORM_BUFFER_OFFSET_ALIGNMENT` 16 is a lookup, not
a probe: Direct3D 11 mandates 256 and every Windows capture measures 256, so
one `getParameter` call and a constant decide it. Every row above is one call
like that.

The serving path is not broken. All six entry points, the whole table and the
fractional map landed in 0103, 0112 and their predecessors and work as
written. What sends Metal down the wrong branch is one predicate:
`RealGLApi::ApostateEnforces()` asks `BackendEnforcesGlLimits()` whether the
backend honours what it reports, Metal honours everything it reports, so the
claim is clamped to the host and the whole anchor is discarded.

The predicate answers its own question correctly. The question is wrong, and
0103 and 0112 both asked it.

What is actually being decided

A numeric GL limit leaves the GPU process through six shared entry points --
plain and robust `glGetIntegerv`, `glGetFloatv`, `glGetInteger64v` -- and three
unrelated consumers come through them:

  a WebGL-compatibility context  the numbers reach a page verbatim, and
                                 nothing else depends on them
  the compositor and raster      Chromium sizes tiles and resources from them
  Skia Ganesh                    ui/gl/init/create_gr_gl_interface.cc binds
                                 Skia's get_integerv straight to gl::GLApi's,
                                 and GrGLCaps reads MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE,
                                 MAX_RENDERBUFFER_SIZE and MAX_SAMPLES

Only the first is a fingerprint. That is why deleting the clamp outright is not
the fix even though it produces the right numbers on the surface anyone
measures: telling Skia a four-sample Metal device does eight samples is not a
coherence trade, it is a rendering defect in canvas2D and raster for every
page, fingerprinted or not, and no page ever reads the number that caused it.
The blast radius, not the fingerprint, is what decides the shape of this patch.

So the gate becomes the kind of context asking. A WebGL-compatibility context
is served the composed claim on every backend; everything else keeps the
intersection of claim and host it has always had, which can lower a cap below
the host's and never raise it, so Skia cannot be told this machine does more
than it does. On an M4 Max under any anchor in the catalogue the intersection
is the host's own value on every row, because an anchor's limits sit at or
above Apple's.

The marker, and why it is not an inference

`GL_ANGLE_webgl_compatibility` is ANGLE's own definition of the thing. ANGLE
sets `extensions->webglCompatibilityANGLE = mWebGLContext` in
`Context::initializeCaps`; `mWebGLContext` is the
`EGL_CONTEXT_WEBGL_COMPATIBILITY_ANGLE` attribute
`ui/gl/gl_context_egl.cc` passes when `GLContextAttribs::
webgl_compatibility_context` is set; and ANGLE's own `State::isWebGL()` is
defined as exactly that extension bit.
`gpu/command_buffer/service/feature_info.cc` asks the same question the same
way, with `gfx::HasExtension(extensions, "GL_ANGLE_webgl_compatibility")`, so
this patch adopts an existing convention rather than inventing one.

Two properties make reading it safe. It is not a requestable extension --
`gles_extensions_autogen.cpp` registers it through `esOnlyExtension`, which
leaves `ExtensionInfo::Requestable` false -- so the
`EGL_EXTENSIONS_ENABLED_ANGLE = false` that Chromium passes for WebGL contexts
does not switch it off, which is the trap a naive extension probe would fall
into. And it is fixed at context creation, so the answer can be cached: one
`RealGLApi` is constructed per `gl::GLContext` in `GLContext::CreateGLApi`, and
the passthrough decoder -- the only thing that ever asks for a
WebGL-compatibility context, in `GenerateGLContextAttribsForDecoder` -- cannot
be combined with virtualised contexts, which `SharedContextState::InitializeGL`
DCHECKs. So one real context never serves a WebGL client and a raster client at
once, and a per-instance flag is a per-context flag.

It is read through `GLApiBase`, not through `RealGLApi::glGetStringFn`, for the
same reason the old renderer read was: the derived entry point substitutes the
profile's strings, and asking the claim about the host answers about the wrong
device.

The validating decoder is the one configuration this leaves behind, and it
leaves it behind exactly as it found it. `GenerateGLContextAttribsForDecoder`
sets `webgl_compatibility_context` only under
`use_passthrough_cmd_decoder()`, so a WebGL context on the validating decoder
was never marked and is served the host's limits. That decoder is not what
ships and not what any measurement here was taken on; it is named rather than
handled.

What is deliberately not changed

ANGLE's `ApplyProfilePointSizeCaps` and `ApplyProfileIntegerCaps` still combine
a profile value with the native cap by taking the lower of the two, so ANGLE's
compiler resources, state validation and format table keep describing the
machine that is really there. That is what keeps the residual honest. Raising
those caps would not remove a single row from the table below; it would convert
a refused call into a wrong render, which is strictly worse.

`ApostateServedLimit`'s floor direction is unchanged -- `MIN_PROGRAM_TEXEL_
OFFSET` is still a floor and an aliased range is still an interval -- and so is
every entry in the table. The boolean it takes changes sense and name, from
`enforces` to `serve_composed`, because the old name described a property of
the backend and the value is now a property of the context.

The per-query `LOG(WARNING)` on the clamp is gone, and this is the second thing
the old axis got wrong. It fired whenever a claim exceeded the host, which on
an M4 Max is most rows, on a code path the compositor and raster hit for every
lookup, to say that "a page that allocates past the real limit would see
through the claim" -- about a context no page can reach. The replacement logs
once, on the serving side, where the statement is true.

What it costs, measured

The residual inverts. It used to be a table a page could read; it is now a set
of operations a page can attempt. Under `windows-d3d11-nvidia` on an M4 Max:

  point size     gl_PointSize above 511 draws a 511-pixel footprint
  viewport       viewport(0,0,32767,32767) then getParameter(VIEWPORT) reads
                 [0,0,16384,16384]; SCISSOR_BOX is the control and is not
                 clamped
  uniforms       a vertex shader declaring 4095 vectors fails to compile or
                 link with the driver's own info log
  samples        renderbufferStorageMultisample at 8 returns
                 GL_INVALID_OPERATION where the Metal format table has 4
  UBO alignment  bindBufferRange at offset 16 SUCCEEDS on the passthrough
                 decoder, because ANGLE validates against its own 16; on the
                 validating decoder ContextGroup's cached 256 rejects it, which
                 is what a real 256-byte-aligned device does

Each is a draw, a compile or a small allocation. Each replaces a constant
lookup. `docs/LIMITATIONS.md` carries the table, the `SCISSOR_BOX` control and
the withdrawal of the v0.1.0 argument that an honest 16384 was worth serving in
preference to the anchor's 32767 -- withdrawn because it priced one row and
ignored that the same gate was discarding six others, and because one honest
row inside a composed cluster is a shape no machine produces.

`ALIASED_LINE_WIDTH_RANGE` is not in that list and `[1, 64]` is not a mistake.
The Windows D3D11 anchors and the Apple anchor all measure `[1, 1]`; the
`linux-vulkan-nvidia` anchor measures `[1, 64]` in all four of its captures,
which is what a Vulkan NVIDIA machine reports, because Direct3D and Metal cap
line width at 1 and Vulkan does not. Under that anchor on a Mac it joins the
list; under a Windows anchor there is nothing to falsify.

MSAA is the row to re-verify after the build, because it is the one that could
break rendering rather than a fingerprint. `DrawingBuffer` takes
`min(preference, MAX_SAMPLES)` with a preference of 4, so a WebGL context
created with `antialias: true` is still allocated at the host's 4 and
`SAMPLES`, `SAMPLE_BUFFERS` and the rendered coverage still agree -- and the
anchor itself measured `MAX_SAMPLES` 8 beside `antialiasSamples` 4, so the
served pair is a pair a real machine reports. Canvas2D goes through Ganesh on a
non-WebGL context and sees the host's caps unchanged. Neither is asserted here:
a throwaway page that draws an antialiased 2D canvas and creates an
`antialias: true` WebGL context, checks both for GL errors and repeats every
read, is left at `/tmp/w1/MetalGate/verify-0119.mjs` for the build that follows
this patch.

The expected outcome of `scripts/checks/gl-caps-check.mjs` on the macOS
artifact after this patch is 5 of 5 -- SERVED and DISTINCT for every anchor,
IDENTITY unchanged -- which is what §6 of the release battery is waiting on. It
is 5 of 5 on linux-x64 already and this patch does not change what a page reads
there: llvmpipe and SwiftShader were already served the claim, and they are
still served it, now because the context asking is WebGL rather than because
the backend enforces nothing. What does change on those backends is the part no
page reads. Skia and the compositor used to get the claim too, since the old
gate was per-process; they now get the intersection, so on a SwiftShader host
GrGLCaps stops being told the device does the anchor's 16 samples when
SwiftShader has 4 and grants 0. That is a rendering correction this patch
picks up on the way past, not the thing it is for.

`--fingerprint-explain` no longer says a limit the host enforces below the
claim is served at the host's value, because it no longer is. The count is
taken from the merged profile as 0112 left it, and it needs no addition for the
fractional limits: the profile carries one `gl_limits` dictionary and
`base/apostate/profile.cc` splits integral from fractional at load time, after
composition, so the count already includes both.

Ledger: webgl.parameters, webgl2.parameters, emit.gl.real-gl-api-get-integerv,
coh.angle-backend-vs-gpu-claim, coh.msaa-samples-vs-render.

